  <h2>How to Set Up Your eSIM (Step by Step)</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Purchase your plan on Airalo — receive a QR code by email</li>
    <li>Go to your phone Settings → Mobile / Cellular Data → Add eSIM</li>
    <li>Scan the QR code (do this before you board — needs Wi-Fi)</li>
    <li>Leave the eSIM in "standby" mode until you land in Korea</li>
    <li>Upon landing, switch the eSIM to active — instant connection</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>eSIM vs Airport SIM Card</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>✅ No waiting in SIM card lines at Incheon (can take 30–60 min in peak season)</li>
    <li>✅ Keep your home SIM active for calls and texts simultaneously</li>
    <li>✅ Usually cheaper than airport counter SIM cards</li>
    <li>✅ Instant delivery — no shipping wait</li>
    <li>⚠️ Requires compatible device</li>
    <li>⚠️ Data only — no Korean phone number for calls</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Airport Wi-Fi Alternative</h2>
  <p>Incheon Airport has free Wi-Fi throughout. If you need to book something on arrival before your eSIM activates, it works fine. KT also has a Pocket Wi-Fi rental counter at the airport if you prefer a physical device for your whole group.</p>

  <h2>Which Data Plan to Choose?</h2>
  <p>For a typical Seoul trip:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>3–5 days:</strong> 1–3GB is enough (maps, messaging, Instagram)</li>
    <li><strong>1 week:</strong> 3–5GB recommended</li>
    <li><strong>2+ weeks:</strong> 10GB or unlimited</li>
    <li><strong>Heavy video/streaming:</strong> Go unlimited to avoid throttling</li>
  </ul>
